{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1475 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "595957dcf0a21f873449fca19ceae1301938aebe5a22b10fafcc14714d35b5ca" :edb/db-before-t 1474 :edb/db-after-t 1475 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534787 50 #inst "2026-10-08T06:52:53.643Z" 13194139534787 true] [17592186166584 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186166584 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\nII. $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" 13194139534787 false] [17592186166585 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186166585 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\nII. $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\nIII. $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" 13194139534787 false] [17592186166745 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310207 13194139534787 true] [17592186166746 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310200 13194139534787 true] [17592186166747 1020 #edb/ref 17592186310193 13194139534787 true] [17592186167378 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534787 false] [17592186167378 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534787 true] [17592186167378 1100 "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+3x{y}^{′}=xy$\n- $3{y}^{″}{y}^{′}-2xy=2{y}^{′}$\n- ${x}^{3}{y}^{′}-2x={y}^{″}$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186167378 1100 "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. $y^{\\prime \\prime \\prime } + 3xy^{\\prime } = xy$\nII. $3y^{\\prime \\prime }y^{\\prime } - 2xy = 2y^{\\prime }$\nIII. $x^{3}y^{\\prime } - 2x = y^{\\prime \\prime }$" 13194139534787 false] [17592186167378 1145 "A differential equation is of third-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴}.$\n\n- Equation II is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a third-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534787 true] [17592186167378 1145 "The order is the highest derivative in each equation. Equation I contains $y'''$ and is third order. Equations II and III contain $y''$ but no third derivative, so both are second order. Select **I only**." 13194139534787 false] [17592186167379 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186167379 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{y}$\nII. $\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3y = 0$\nIII. $2\\frac{dy}{dx} - xy = \\sqrt{x}$" 13194139534787 false] [17592186167379 1145 "An autonomous first-order ODE can be written as $y'=f(y)$ with no explicit $x$ dependence.\n\n- I is already $y'=e^y$.\n- II becomes $y'=3y$.\n- III becomes $y'=(xy+\\sqrt x)/2$, which depends explicitly on $x$.\n\nSelect **I and II only**." 13194139534787 false] [17592186167379 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534787 true] [17592186167405 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534787 false] [17592186167405 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534787 true] [17592186167405 1100 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy={e}^{x}$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186167405 1100 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. $y^{\\prime } = e^{x}$\nII. $y^{\\prime } - \\frac{3}{y} = 0$\nIII. $\\frac{1}{2}y^{\\prime } - xy = e^{x}$" 13194139534787 false] [17592186167405 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′} & =xy+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2(xy+{e}^{x}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2(xy+{e}^{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139534787 true] [17592186167405 1145 "Isolate $y'$ in each equation. I gives $y'=e^x$ and III gives $y'=2xy+2e^x$, so both depend explicitly on $x$. II gives $y'=3/y$, depending only on $y$. Therefore the equations that are **not** autonomous are **I and III only**." 13194139534787 false] [17592186257268 1017 #edb/ref 17592186274341 13194139534787 false] [17592186257268 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310187 13194139534787 true] [17592186262013 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262014 13194139534787 false] [17592186262013 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310214 13194139534787 true] [17592186262013 1100 "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x){y}^{′}={x}^{2}y$\n- $5{y}^{″}{y}^{′}+3xy=4{y}^{′}$\n- ${({y}^{′})}^{2}-2{x}^{2}=y$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186262013 1100 "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. ${y}^{‴}+(\\operatorname{sin}x){y}^{'}={x}^{2}y$\nII. $5{y}^{''}{y}^{'}+3xy=4{y}^{'}$\nIII. ${({y}^{'})}^{2}-2{x}^{2}=y$" 13194139534787 false] [17592186262013 1145 "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534787 false] [17592186262013 1145 "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{″},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{′}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139534787 true] [17592186262063 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262064 13194139534787 false] [17592186262063 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310220 13194139534787 true] [17592186262063 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186262063 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\nII. $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}y$\nIII. $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" 13194139534787 false] [17592186262063 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534787 false] [17592186262063 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139534787 true] [17592186262093 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262094 13194139534787 false] [17592186262093 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310226 13194139534787 true] [17592186262093 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186262093 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\nII. $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\nIII. ${y}^{'}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" 13194139534787 false] [17592186262093 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534787 false] [17592186262093 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534787 true] [17592186281012 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186281012 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\nII. ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\nIII. $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" 13194139534787 false] [17592186281047 1100 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186281047 1100 "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\nII. ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\nIII. ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" 13194139534787 false] [17592186310187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310188 13194139534787 true] [17592186310187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310189 13194139534787 true] [17592186310187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310190 13194139534787 true] [17592186310187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310191 13194139534787 true] [17592186310187 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310192 13194139534787 true] [17592186310187 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534787 true] [17592186310187 1041 "selection" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310187 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310189 13194139534787 true] [17592186310187 1110 #uuid "5708abd5-c553-57d5-b6da-58776b034e7e" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310188 1034 "9" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310188 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534787 true] [17592186310188 1111 #uuid "081ddc59-8da5-572e-a067-ebfe7fee2456" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310189 1034 "8" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310189 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534787 true] [17592186310189 1111 #uuid "31828460-6eba-5650-8722-c0e1db601224" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310190 1034 "7" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310190 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534787 true] [17592186310190 1111 #uuid "e3fc8b29-5ef2-58c0-807d-54dae19b8ecd" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310191 1034 "2" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310191 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534787 true] [17592186310191 1111 #uuid "ec559370-184b-5d00-8c7f-d07ff6788d86" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310192 1034 "6" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310192 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534787 true] [17592186310192 1111 #uuid "2eced7cd-c016-56e3-a405-f3d575b9fbd1" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310193 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310194 13194139534787 true] [17592186310193 1089 "q-118788" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310193 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534787 true] [17592186310193 1100 "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $6{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310193 1113 #uuid "e81dead2-8026-5bbf-8274-50b6fe156c6d" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310193 1145 "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310195 13194139534787 true] [17592186310194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310196 13194139534787 true] [17592186310194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310197 13194139534787 true] [17592186310194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310198 13194139534787 true] [17592186310194 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310199 13194139534787 true] [17592186310194 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534787 true] [17592186310194 1041 "selection" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310194 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310199 13194139534787 true] [17592186310194 1110 #uuid "023a2699-f925-5c39-8ed3-1368ca4c51ef" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310195 1034 "I and III only" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310195 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534787 true] [17592186310195 1111 #uuid "819837bc-3c26-5982-be1c-aba248d960bf" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310196 1034 "III only" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310196 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534787 true] [17592186310196 1111 #uuid "0847046b-ab2c-5c03-9d24-c78203613c35" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310197 1034 "I and II only" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310197 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534787 true] [17592186310197 1111 #uuid "7aff5497-9742-5c3b-ba29-c7220a0e52c1" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310198 1034 "None of those listed" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310198 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534787 true] [17592186310198 1111 #uuid "ff03baea-fc93-5382-974b-f89e934de061" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310199 1034 "II only" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310199 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139534787 true] [17592186310199 1111 #uuid "cb518d2b-76e8-5383-9317-12e231c1d909" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310200 1017 #edb/ref 17592186310201 13194139534787 true] [17592186310200 1089 "q-118859" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310200 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534787 true] [17592186310200 1100 "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-3{x}^{2}=1$\n- ${y}^{′}+6y={e}^{3y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310200 1113 #uuid "6fdef7eb-2d32-5a05-8afa-f9593109442c" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310200 1145 "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+1.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+6y & ={e}^{3y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={e}^{3y}-6y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={e}^{3y}-6y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310202 13194139534787 true] [17592186310201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310203 13194139534787 true] [17592186310201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310204 13194139534787 true] [17592186310201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310205 13194139534787 true] [17592186310201 1000 #edb/ref 17592186310206 13194139534787 true] [17592186310201 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534787 true] [17592186310201 1041 "selection" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310201 1104 #edb/ref 17592186310206 13194139534787 true] [17592186310201 1110 #uuid "41e36352-b8f1-5f96-8328-34f73f65a743" 13194139534787 true] [17592186310202 1034 "I, II, and III" 1319